{"schema":"statespace/lesson@1","id":"S3.L5","tier":"bridge","title":"What progress and preservation buy","draft":true,"kcs":["KC-3.progress-preservation-scope"],"objective":"Read the progress and preservation statements beside the step-invariant theorem, then judge candidate guarantees true or false, separating what type safety gives from the business invariants it does not.","segments":[{"id":"s1","title":"A question first","minutes":3,"kcs":["KC-3.progress-preservation-scope"],"blocks":[{"type":"prose","md":"Type safety is the headline result of most language courses. Here is what it is worth to a ledger."},{"type":"item","role":"prequestion","family":"GEN-call-it","template":"t1","kcs":["KC-3.progress-preservation-scope"],"choice":{"prompt":"Progress and preservation are proved for Mini-Plinth. Do those two theorems alone guarantee that well-typed Transfers keep balances non-negative?","options":[{"id":"A","text":"Yes: that is what type safety means for a balance.","why":"Type safety is about steps and types, not about what a balance holds.","mis":"dp:S4"},{"id":"B","text":"No: they rule out stuck states, not business violations.","why":"The theorems speak of steps and types. R-cover and the step invariant carry balances."},{"id":"C","text":"Yes, but only for programs that terminate.","why":"Termination is a separate property.","mis":"dp:S8"}],"key":"B"},"itemRev":"bd78fab00717dd25"}]},{"id":"s2","title":"Two theorems about steps","minutes":11,"kcs":["KC-3.progress-preservation-scope"],"blocks":[{"type":"prose","md":"Type safety is two theorems. Progress says a well-typed configuration is either final or can take a step. Final means `done` or `err`, so a well-typed configuration is never stuck. Preservation says a step from a well-typed configuration lands on a well-typed one. Used together, a well-typed program is never stuck at any point of its run: each step keeps it well-typed, and a well-typed configuration that is not final always has a next step.\n\nThat is a real guarantee, and it has a precise edge. Both theorems are about steps and types. Neither mentions a balance, a hold's status or a guard's outcome. A well-typed call can still end in an error, because an error is a defined outcome and not a stuck state. The previous lesson drew that line.\n\nThe step invariant is a different kind of statement. It is about a state, and it needs R-cover, through a lemma named `covers_sound`, before it can say anything about a balance. Read the three side by side and ask what each one mentions."},{"type":"instrument","ins":"INS-goal-replay","cell":1,"callOnFirstRun":true,"recorded":"RUN-lean-progress","md":"The statements, as the reference states them.","direction":"Goal Replay shows the statements read-only. None of them is yours to edit here.","code":{"lang":"lean","text":"theorem progress     {c : Cfg}    : Typed c → Final c ∨ ∃ c', Step c c'\ntheorem preservation {c c' : Cfg} : Typed c → Step c c' → Typed c'\n\ntheorem step_preserves_inv :\n    p.WellTyped → Inv s → exec p c s = .ok (s', o, es) → Inv s'"},"call":{"prompt":"Delete R-cover from the typing rules. Which theorem stops being provable?","options":[{"id":"A","text":"`progress`: an unguarded debit gets stuck.","why":"An unguarded debit still steps. It only drives a balance negative.","mis":"dp:S4"},{"id":"B","text":"`preservation`: an unguarded debit loses its types.","why":"An unguarded debit keeps its types.","mis":"dp:S4"},{"id":"C","text":"Neither: `step_preserves_inv` is the one that fails.","why":"Only the step invariant mentions balances."},{"id":"D","text":"Both: each depends on R-cover.","why":"Neither theorem depends on R-cover.","mis":"dp:S9"}],"key":"C","keyRun":"RUN-lean-progress","surprise":"yes"},"after":"Without R-cover an unguarded debit still takes a step and its types still line up, so progress and preservation stand {{run:RUN-lean-progress}} {{run:RUN-lean-preservation}}. What breaks is `step_preserves_inv`: the move can drive a balance below zero, and that theorem alone speaks of balances.\n\nYour own system has the same split. A typed API layer rules out whole families of mistakes before a request arrives, and a balance below zero is not one of them. That takes a guard, a check in the database or a proof like this stage's, and each is a separate piece of evidence. The overclaim to watch for is \"the types check, so the ledger works.\"","itemRev":"04251b3692548cfb"},{"type":"card","variant":"twin","title":"Production twin","md":"Cedar's Lean model proves `typecheck_is_sound`: when typechecking succeeds, evaluation produces a boolean or one of a short list of errors, `entityDoesNotExist`, `extensionError` and `arithBoundsError`. The typechecker \"has no knowledge of the entities/context that will be provided at authorization time\". Read it in `cedar-lean/Cedar/Thm/Typechecking.lean` (read 2026-10-23, pinned in {{run:RUN-twin-pins}}). Mini-Plinth's analogue: a well-typed call can still end in `unknownAccount`, `unknownHold`, `holdNotOpen` or `guardFailed`.","runs":["RUN-twin-pins"]},{"type":"item","role":"segment","family":"GEN-call-it","template":"t4","kcs":["KC-3.progress-preservation-scope"],"choice":{"prompt":"Which of these guarantees follows from progress and preservation for well-typed Mini-Plinth programs?","options":[{"id":"A","text":"A well-typed call never gets stuck.","why":"That is progress, applied after every step because preservation keeps the program well-typed."},{"id":"B","text":"A well-typed call never returns an error.","why":"Well-typed calls still end in `unknownAccount`, `guardFailed` and the like.","mis":"dp:S7"},{"id":"C","text":"Every well-typed call keeps the total balance at zero.","why":"Conservation is a claim about states. It needs the step invariant.","mis":"dp:S4"},{"id":"D","text":"A well-typed `capture` never returns `holdNotOpen`.","why":"A hold's status lives in the state, which typing does not see.","mis":"dp:S4"}],"key":"A"},"itemRev":"38ae3e7a4a7af128"}]},{"id":"s3","title":"Exit","minutes":4,"kcs":["KC-3.progress-preservation-scope"],"blocks":[{"type":"item","role":"exit","family":"GEN-proof-structure","template":"t1","kcs":["KC-3.progress-preservation-scope"],"choice":{"prompt":"A preservation proof for the expression fragment goes by induction on the step. In the case where `balance(e)` steps to `balance(e')`, you hold `hstep : Step s e e'` and `ih : ∀ {t : Ty}, HasTy e t → HasTy e' t`, and you have introduced `ht : HasTy e.bal t`, with goal `HasTy e'.bal t`. After `cases ht with | bal h`, which goal is left?","options":[{"id":"A","text":"`h : HasTy e Ty.account ⊢ HasTy e'.bal Ty.int`","why":"The typing rule for `balance` gives `h` for the inner expression and fixes the type to `Ty.int`. The goal stays about the stepped expression."},{"id":"B","text":"`h : HasTy e Ty.account ⊢ HasTy e' Ty.int`","why":"The stepped expression is still `balance(e')`, not `e'`.","mis":"dp:L4"},{"id":"C","text":"`h : HasTy e Ty.account ⊢ HasTy e'.bal Ty.account`","why":"`balance` returns a number, so the type is `Ty.int`.","mis":"dp:L4"},{"id":"D","text":"`h : Step s e e' ⊢ HasTy e'.bal Ty.int`","why":"`h` comes from the typing derivation, and `hstep` is the step.","mis":"dp:L4"}],"key":"A","keyRun":"RUN-lean-preservation"},"itemRev":"fada43cd3b292f98"},{"type":"claim","template":"CL-3.1"},{"type":"claim","template":"CL-3.2"},{"type":"card","variant":"reading","title":"Reading","md":"Read 2026-10-23. Harper, *Practical Foundations for Programming Languages*, section 6.1 \"Preservation\" and section 6.2 \"Progress\", and the Cedar file named above."},{"type":"card","variant":"build-this","title":"Build this","md":"Start the step invariant with the per-operation lemmas. The stretch stages, LAB-3.12 and LAB-3.13, prove progress and preservation for the fragment.","lab":"LAB-3.4","minutes":45}]}],"minutes":18,"rail":[{"id":"s1","title":"A question first"},{"id":"s2","title":"Two theorems about steps"},{"id":"s3","title":"Exit"}],"establishes":{"claims":["CL-3.1","CL-3.2"],"specimens":[]},"runs":{"RUN-lean-preservation":{"id":"RUN-lean-preservation","title":"Preservation for the template's small-step relation (steps keep typing), under any policy, accepted by comparator; with R-cover deleted from the reference type checker it still holds","engine":"lean","version":"4.35.0","binary_sha256":"bf8d54e4714cc4b03d3f6bb34c83b7202b87e49c8bfcbff6895c085bb90ceb38","image":"statespace-bench@sha256:701be043775cc261e84a92fe985ef94d524617fdb3d53e2255187932d2ec0f9a","argv":[["docker","run","--rm","--name","ssrec-run-lean-preservation-comparator","--memory=6g","--network","none","-v","<sandbox>/work:/w","-w","/w","statespace-bench@sha256:701be043775cc261e84a92fe985ef94d524617fdb3d53e2255187932d2ec0f9a","sh","-c","lake build Lab3Stretch.Safety >/dev/null 2>&1 || lake build Lab3Stretch.Safety && lake env lean Axioms.lean && lake comparator --config comparator-preservation.json --inadvisably-no-sandbox"],["docker","run","--rm","--name","ssrec-run-lean-preservation-no-rcover","--memory=6g","--network","none","-v","<sandbox>/work:/w","-w","/w","statespace-bench@sha256:701be043775cc261e84a92fe985ef94d524617fdb3d53e2255187932d2ec0f9a","sh","-c","lake build Lab3Stretch.Safety >/dev/null 2>&1 || lake build Lab3Stretch.Safety && lake env lean Axioms.lean"]],"inputs":[{"path":"labs/LAB-3/ExportIR.lean","sha256":"01bd06e7e92683097b2dab4026379d67f371201643f39c4f149fd3010a1d9891"},{"path":"labs/LAB-3/Lab3/Eval.lean","sha256":"689b947ae307ba278b3d87ac7f5e31551f1996aa01a974fc275801d3e9f06659"},{"path":"labs/LAB-3/Lab3/Export.lean","sha256":"449019ce359971d2b0d50b500e10ad6904d18ddbd653924448a5dd21b9144604"},{"path":"labs/LAB-3/Lab3/Proofs.lean","sha256":"be9b3cbddcf64a81328121fbed2dce2d557dc87d4e658aa25fe5f06107ecba44"},{"path":"labs/LAB-3/Lab3/Syntax.lean","sha256":"6ce5e8d198e34068746b1a8af4cc5f56d61292d43f9acd6942328cdaa8af9112"},{"path":"labs/LAB-3/Lab3/Typing.lean","sha256":"87504c73057c821e1367ccd08fa22ddc27fa1e93751455e48ce100cb8114578e"}